## Part 2 — Additional commercial terms

> **These terms are supplementary conditions layered on top of the AGPL-3.0.
> Where they conflict with the AGPL-3.0, these terms prevail — but only with
> respect to the commercial-authorization scenarios described below. They do
> not remove, reduce, or replace any right that the AGPL-3.0 grants you, and
> they do not alter the AGPL-3.0 in any other respect.**

The purpose of this Part is narrow and specific: to state clearly **when a
closed-source use is free of charge, and when it requires a paid license**.

### 2.1 Open-source use — always free

If you use this software, or any derivative work of it, **in full compliance
with the AGPL-3.0** — meaning you keep the source code open and you release
all your modifications under the AGPL-3.0 as well — then you owe **no license
fee whatsoever**, regardless of how large your organization is or how much
revenue you earn.

You do not need to contact the copyright holder. You do not need a written
agreement. The AGPL-3.0 alone is sufficient.

### 2.2 Closed-source use, small scale — free

If you choose **not** to comply with the AGPL-3.0's source-disclosure
requirement — that is, you distribute the software in closed-source form, or
you operate it as a closed-source network service — **and** the total revenue
described in §2.4 is **below CNY 10,000,000** (ten million Chinese yuan, or the
equivalent in another currency) for your most recently completed fiscal
quarter, then you are **automatically granted a free, no-fee license for that
closed-source use**.

No application, no registration, and no contact with the copyright holder is
required for this exemption.

### 2.3 Closed-source use, large scale — paid license required

If you choose closed-source use **and** the total revenue described in §2.4 is
**at or above CNY 10,000,000** for your most recently completed fiscal quarter,
then you **must contact the copyright holder** (see Part 3) and obtain a
**separate written commercial license** before using the software in that way.

Using the software in this scenario without such a written license is an
infringement of copyright, and the user bears the corresponding legal
liability.

### 2.4 Definitions used in this Part

- **"Total revenue"** means the entire revenue your organization (or the legal
  entity you represent) received from **all** of its business activities during
  its most recently completed fiscal quarter. It is **not** limited to revenue
  derived from this software.
- If you operate several projects or products, their revenues are **aggregated**
  for the purpose of the threshold above.
- **"Most recently completed fiscal quarter"** means the last full quarter for
  which your organization has closed its books. If your organization has not
  completed a fiscal quarter (for example, a newly formed entity), the
  threshold is assessed on a pro-rata basis until one full quarter has closed.
- **"You"** includes both individuals and legal entities, and includes anyone
  acting on an entity's behalf.

### 2.5 Why these terms exist

The AGPL-3.0 already permits commercial use — but only on the condition that
the source stays open. This Part exists so that a company which *cannot* or
*does not want to* open its source still has a clear, predictable path: free
below the threshold, negotiable above it. The intent is to make the common case
easy and the large-scale case explicit, rather than to restrict anyone's
existing AGPL rights.

